2D tensile rod test case, for plasticity and fragmentation/damage models.
The test case is set up in dimensionless units.
See Gray (2001), Gray et al. (2001), and Schäfer (2005) for details.
See also Schäfer et al. (2016), Sect. 4.1 for the used units.

A constant velocity (5e-3) for slices of particles pulls the rod apart, defining the strain rate.
These boundary conditions are implemented directly in boundary.cu. In addition, a transition zone
without flaws ensures that the elastic wave generated by the boundary conditions is transmitted correctly.

Particles are placed on a squared grid, the resolution can be set via the grid
spacing (see input/create_2D_rod.py -h). Don't forget to adapt the sml in material.cfg.
The sml may be around 3x the grid spacing, and may depend on the strain rate.

The Weibull parameters represent a basalt-like material, see Schäfer (2005).


How to run it:

(i)   run input/create_2D_rod.py to create initial conditions (no flags necessary for default settings)
      copy input/rod.0000 to this directory
(ii)  compile miluphcuda with src/boundary.cu and src/parameter.h
(iii) execute run.sh
(iv)  wait
(v)   run mk_animation.sh to visualize the results

The simulation typically runs for few minutes and produces 250 output files (8 MB each).


The results/ directory contains accuracy-converged animations of the crack growth process,
with the regular particle spacing of 0.0125 cm and in higher resolution with 0.005 cm.
They were produced with an rk2_adaptive eps = 1e-6 and MAX_DAMAGE_CHANGE = 0.01.


Christoph Schaefer, Christoph Burger
ch.schaefer@uni-tuebingen.de

last updated: 2021-05-22



Test of the von Mises radial return
-----------------------------------

The von Mises model uses the radial return f = Y/sqrt(3 J2) (Wilkins 1964) instead of f = Y^2/(3 J2) of
Benz & Asphaug (1994, 1995), see src/plasticity.cu. The squared form softens the material in plastic flow by an
amount of first order in the time step. To see the difference, run the rod without fragmentation:

(i)   in src/parameter.h set FRAGMENTATION 0, DAMAGE_ACTS_ON_S 0, MAX_NUM_FLAWS 1 (VON_MISES_PLASTICITY stays 1)
(ii)  input/create_2D_rod.py --no_flaws
(iii) compile and run with fixed upper limits of the time step, e.g. -M 2e-3, 1e-3, 5e-4 (add -M to run.sh,
      one output directory per run), with the current code and with the old squared form
(iv)  ./check_von_mises.py --yield_stress 0.03 rod.0100.h5 rod.0200.h5   (in each run directory)

Expected: with the radial return, the stress entering the forces is Y in plastic flow independent of the time step,
and rod elongation, necking and total plastic strain do not change with -M. With the squared form, the limited stress
lies below Y by about half the elastic stress increment per step, and the results converge to those of the radial
return only for -M -> 0.
